WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
You know Faker as the greatest player to ever play League of Legends.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:08.200
But in 2015, the most exciting mid laner in the scene was spending a lot of his time playing

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.480
from behind a substitute.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.380
In interviews, Faker said he was willing to do whatever was best for the team.

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.580
But actions speak louder than words.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:29.560
As 2015 began Faker is coming off a season that saw him try and fail to carry SKT to Worlds.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.960
But if anything that season showed how incredible a player Faker really is

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.440
even when the situation around him is unstable.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:40.820
Near the end of that year Riot released Azir. While the champion was released in September he

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:45.000
wasn't enabled for Worlds. But Azir's intricate design made fans wonder

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.740
how exactly Faker would take advantage of it. Between two of his accounts

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:53.100
Faker played 42 games on Azir in 2014 and while his win rate wasn't all that

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.380
high he did make some highlights.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.840
The rules for the 2015 LCK season got rid of sister teams.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.520
So Faker and Bengi joined MaRin, Wolf and Bang from T1 S.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.080
But the mid laner from S, Easyhoon, also made the switch.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.100
And as the meta started to shift from assassins to control mages,

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.720
it became clear that Faker was gonna have to share time

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.080
even at the beginning of the split.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:43.900
"Wait, wait what's this? What's this I spy in the SKT booth, it's not Faker."

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.760
"It's Easyhoon. Faker, here's how we operate. We pick our star player into an unfavorable match and we kick

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.600
him out when he doesn't do well."

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:00.140
In the LCK, Faker spent most of his time playing champions like Ezreal, Ahri and LeBlanc.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.600
Meanwhily Easyhoon was playing champions like Lulu, Xerath and Cassiopeia.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.780
But outside of the LCK, Faker racked up games on Azir in solo queue.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:14.020
He played 130 games on the champion in 2015. More than any other champion he played.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:17.160
For someone who's substitute was supposed to be handling control mages

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.840
Faker was spending a lot of his time trying to master one.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.480
But in week six of the season. Faker finally got a chance to show off his Azir.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.080
"We may finally get to see Faker's Azir. This would be very exciting."

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.080
"We've seen a lot of clips of him from solo queue."

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:31.440
"It's happening."

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.800
It wasn't exactly the debut fans were expecting.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:03:33.800
(Casting)

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.880
As both mid laners continue to share time, SKT finished second in the regular season.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.900
Faker played 20 games and Easyhoon played 12.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.360
In the playoffs, Faker was a key part of SKT’s reverse sweep against CJ Entus.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.680
After Easyhoon played in the Game 1 loss, Faker stepped in.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.080
But when it came time for the grand finals against the GE Tigers,

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:59.040
Faker sat out the entire series with Easyhoon, playing mid with Cassiopeia and Azir, to sweep their opponents.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:07.360
(Casting)

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:11.460
Faker took the opportunity in a post-game interview to say he was happy that his team won,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.860
but he was willing to do whatever it took to make sure he could play in key series in the future.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:20.340
As SKT prepared for MSI. The question of their mid-lane still remained.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:25.240
But with days to go before the tournament, Faker was still practicing Azir in solo queue.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:30.560
At MSI, Faker got another chance to pull out the Azir. But maybe in the least risky way possible.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.360
Against tournament underdogs, Beşiktaş e-Sports Club.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:36.360
Still it was his birthday and fans ate it up.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:46.840
(Casting)

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.420
Faker got to play Azir two more times that tournament.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:54.240
But by the time Grand Finals rolled around, he was riding the bench for the first three games against EDG.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:05:00.100
However, they put Faker back in with Kassadin for Game 4, forced a Game 5, and eventually his undefeated

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.280
LeBlanc was countered in the final game of the series.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:24.080
(Casting)

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:30.900
Returning to LCK for the summer, the narrative about Faker was still the same, he was an incredible talent.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.620
But there were things Easyhoon could do that Faker just couldn't.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:51.200
(Casting)

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.540
Faker was playing more games but Easyhoon was still considered the better Azir player.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.340
Even though Faker was grinding out games in solo queue.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:02.460
Faker didn't get another chance to show how his Azir had progressed until week seven.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:06.740
Playing a 0-9 SBENU Sonicboom. Faker played Azir into Vel'Koz,

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:09.440
and he picked up his first LCK win on Azir.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.440
But, just barely.

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:17.360
(Casting)

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:26.340
(Casting)

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:32.360
(Casting)

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:37.120
(Casting)

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:43.000
(Casting)

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:46.280
Strangely this seems to have been the turning point for Faker's Azir.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:50.280
He played Azir two more times that season. Going up against the KOO Tigers and CJ Entus.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:56.260
Winning both times. As SKT entered the playoffs in first place they only had to beat KT Rolster.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:00.180
This time Easyhoon rode the bench as Faker played all three games

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.980
playing Riven, Diana… and Azir.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:29.640
(Casting)

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:34.720
SKT brought Easyhoon to Worlds but Faker still played the lion's share of the games the tournament

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.300
The Juggernaut patch that hit before Worlds though

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:40.500
changed a lot of the pick ban priority and Faker played a lot more

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:46.700
Ryze than he did Azir. And in their grand final series against the KOO Tigers. It was Faker, not Easyhoon,

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.980
who was in for every single game.

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:55.740
Easyhoon left for China after 2015, and noted that while he was willing to share time and make sacrifices for SKT

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:59.660
the shadow of the Unkillable Demon King was a lot colder than he expected.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:03.740
And as it turned out, Faker picked a pretty good time to get good at Azir.

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:07.760
In 2016 he became known for his unbelievable plays on the champion.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:14.600
(Casting)

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:32.480
(Casting)

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:37.680
(Casting)

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:42.959
(Casting)

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:47.320
(Casting)

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.840
Even though 2015's control mage meta was perfect for Easyhoon.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:56.420
Faker had surpassed him. Even the greats in League of Legends have bad metas or picks they struggle with.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:59.860
But Faker set out to prove that those limits don't apply to him.

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:00.840
And he did.

